\title{Soundness and Completeness of a Circuit}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-24}

\p{
    Circuit soundness and [protocol soundness](dhsorens-002F) are statements about different objects, and the distinction is worth holding onto because both are called soundness. A protocol theorem says that a prover who convinces the verifier knows a satisfying assignment to some constraint system. A circuit theorem says what a satisfying assignment to \em{that} system implies about the computation the system was supposed to encode. Neither implies the other, and a proof system with both is the only kind whose end-to-end claim mentions the program the user cared about.
}

\p{
    Soundness in Clean quantifies over arbitrary environments. For every offset, every assignment of field elements to cells — no honesty assumed — and every input, if the assumptions hold on the input and the constraints hold under that assignment, then the spec holds of the input and the circuit's output. Quantifying over all environments is what makes this a statement about a cheating prover: the witness cells are adversarial, and the constraints are the only thing the proof has to work with.
}

\p{
    Completeness quantifies over prover environments that \em{use the circuit's own witness generators} — the predicate \code{UsesLocalWitnessesCompleteness} says the environment's cells agree, operation by operation, with what each \code{witness} step computes. Given assumptions, the constraints hold. So completeness is exactly the claim that the generator's output satisfies the constraints on every admissible input, and it is the obligation that fails when a circuit is over-constrained rather than under-constrained. The proof obligation splits differently at subcircuits too: soundness may assume a subcircuit's spec, while completeness must establish its prover-side assumptions.
}

\p{
    Three structures package the pair, and the differences between them are the interesting part of the design. \code{FormalCircuit} is the function-like case: a spec relating input to output, and constraints that are always satisfiable given the assumptions, so the spec is permitted to be strictly weaker than the constraints. \code{FormalAssertion} is the case with no output, where the circuit's purpose is to constrain its input further. Its constraints need not be satisfiable, so completeness is weakened to \em{assumptions and spec imply constraints} — and the consequence is that an assertion's spec must be an equivalent reformulation of its constraints, not a weakening of them.
}

\p{
    \code{GeneralFormalCircuit} is for circuits that are a mix of the two, and it decouples the statements outright: \code{Assumptions} and \code{Spec} appear only in soundness, and separate \code{ProverAssumptions} and \code{ProverSpec} only in completeness. The library's own motivating example is the bit-decomposition gadget. Decomposing #{x} into #{n} bits constrains #{x} to be #{n}-bit representable as a side effect, so representability is a necessary assumption for completeness and an unnecessary one for soundness. Stating it as a \code{FormalCircuit} would push the assumption into the soundness statement and make the gadget useless as a range check, because it would then require the very property it establishes. A fourth structure, \code{DeterministicFormalCircuit}, adds a uniqueness obligation: the spec determines the output, so the constraints cannot leave it ambiguous.
}

\p{
    Soundness carries a second conjunct that is easy to overlook. Alongside the spec, the circuit must discharge the \code{Requirements} of every channel interaction it emits — the obligations a component takes on when it pushes to a channel, as opposed to the guarantees it may assume when it pulls. At the level of a single circuit these are assumed and re-exported; establishing them unconditionally is a property of the whole [ensemble](dhsorens-004W), and it does not follow from the circuit-level proofs.
}

\p{
    What is not yet stated anywhere in this stack is the composite theorem. [Completeness](dhsorens-004T) at the protocol layer is preserved by compilation and is rarely where a system fails; circuit completeness is a different obligation, and the flat AIR ensemble layer marks it as unproved. Until circuit-level and protocol-level statements are joined, a verified circuit and a verified argument are two theorems about the same system with a gap between them, and the gap is exactly the arithmetisation's satisfiability relation.
}
